// synth_proposer.mn — Hμ.synth-proposer peer handle
//
// The oracle's exploration substrate. Replaces the DELETED OneShot
// enumeration stub (it returned []) with real MultiShot
// candidate enumeration over the constraint space. This is what
// makes `??` THE developer's primary write-mode verb (SYNTAX §Partial
// application — one absence marker across space and time): with this
// handler installed, every `??` position invokes
// the proposer chain; the gradient narrows the candidate space; the
// surviving candidate IS the synthesized residue.
//
// Per PLAN §2's cached cursor: the oracle's runtime IS IC + Pure-
// over-broader-input + the multi-shot resume primitive composed.
// This file is that composition; the kernel does the work.
//
// Anchor 0 dream code. Wheel canonical IS the ULTIMATE FORM.
// Verification by simulation, walkthrough, audit. lux3.wasm is not
// the arbiter. The seed transcribes after first-light-L1 + H7
// MultiShot emit per Tier 3 growth pattern.
//
// Anchor 4 — build the wheel; never wrap the axle. The proposer
// chain (enumerative / smt / llm-handler / user-fed) is FOUR peer
// handlers on the SAME Synth effect; swap the chain, swap the
// proposer; the verification stays in the kernel.
//
// Composition tally — every read is from substrate that exists today:
//
//   Synth effect (3 ops; OneShot/MultiShot mix)  — src/mentl.mn:89-93
//   AnnotationSuggestion / Cursor                — src/types.mn (Hμ.cursor)
//   graph_push_checkpoint / graph_rollback        — src/graph.mn + types.mn
//   verify_after_apply (the proof gate)           — src/mentl.mn:277-286
//   Reason chain (per-candidate provenance)       — src/types.mn Reason ADT
//   MultiShot resume discipline                   — PLAN §2's cached cursor
//   Pure-over-broader-input enumeration           — primitive #4 row algebra
//
// Drift modes refused per edit site:
//
//   Drift 1 (vtable):       Synth handler chain via ~> composition;
//                           no fn-pointer table for proposer dispatch.
//   Drift 2 (env frame):    Candidate state is the proposer's
//                           handler-state, NOT a parallel enumeration
//                           frame stack.
//   Drift 4 (MTL):          ONE Synth effect + multiple peer handlers
//                           on it; not stacked single-concern wrappers.
//   Drift 6 (special):      Each candidate (Empty/SuggestEffectRow/
//                           Constructor-fill/Lambda-shape) goes through
//                           the SAME verify_candidate gate; no special-
//                           case for "easy" cases.
//   Drift 7 (parallel arr): Candidate is one record-shape with Node +
//                           Reason; not parallel (asts[], reasons[]).
//   Drift 8 (string flag):  ProposerKind is a tagged ADT.
//   Drift 9 (deferred):     The chain is named in full; each proposer
//                           bodied; BOTH arms live — propose (the
//                           OneShot survivor-set) and
//                           enumerate_inhabitants (the multi-shot
//                           resume-per-proven fan, gated by its own
//                           frontier fixture; the fork pair =
//                           graph checkpoint + heap region).
import types

import effects

import graph

import own

import verify

import mentl

import parser

import infer

import lower

import egraph

import cursor

import query

// ─── ProposerKind — which proposer in the chain emitted a candidate ─
type ProposerKind
  // type-directed enumeration over the constraint space
  = ProposerEnumerative
  // SMT solver via Verify-handler-swap (Arc F.1)
  | ProposerSMT
  // wrap-handler suggestions per H5 AWrapHandler
  | ProposerHandlerCatalog
  // user-supplied seeds via teach_synthesize
  | ProposerUser

// ─── EnrichedCandidate — Candidate + provenance ────────────────────
//
// The Synth effect's `propose` returns [Node]; this file's chain
// wraps each Node with its proposer of origin. Drift 7 closure: ONE
// record-shape, never parallel arrays. RANK IS A PROJECTION, NOT A
// FIELD (Hβ.synth.rank-is-a-projection-not-a-field, resolved): the
// stored cost field compared a measured proximity against invented
// enumerator constants (a vocabulary call genuinely ranking 0.25 lost
// to a literal's made-up 0.3) and rode stale when extraction swapped
// the node — `rank_of` now reads the LIVE node at sort time, once per
// candidate, through the same env/refs reads the gradient owns.
type EnrichedCandidate = EnrichedCandidate({node: Node, origin: ProposerKind, reason: Reason})

// AST fragment
//
// why this was proposed
//
// which proposer emitted
//
// ─── synth_default — the canonical proposer chain entry point ──────
//
// Composes the four proposer handlers in priority order:
//
//   user-fed seeds (rank 1.0) · the handler catalog (0.9) ·
//   refinement-aware SMT (0.7) · type-directed enumeration (0.5)
//   — each a `~>`-installed Synth peer, named, post-first-light.
//
// Each proposer's arm calls verify_candidate to gate its output;
// only proven candidates reach the parent. The chain composes via
// `~>` per primitive #2 (handlers); same kernel; one effect.
//
// Per PLAN §1: dead variants WIDEN the
// constraint space at proof-search time, NOT teach Mentl wrong
// vocabulary. Each proposer emits CANDIDATES; the kernel's
// verify_after_apply is the proof gate.
handler synth_default {
  synth_propose(target_ty, allowed_effects, context) => // The in-scope VOCABULARY first (PLAN §1's second moat: type-directed
  // search over what is already in scope, pruned by the row at every
  // step), then the shape enumerators; every candidate through the ONE
  // proof gate. Row-rejected vocabulary keeps its Reasons.
  // The candidate space's own frontier, read BEFORE any enumeration
  // mints into it: the form half saturates exactly what the fan made
  // (PLAN §5's fork/merge duality — the range is what keeps merging
  // cheap at a cursor position).
  match graph_next() {
    cand_start => match enumerate_vocabulary_calls(target_ty, allowed_effects, context) {
      (vocab, rejected) => {
        let enriched = concat_candidates(vocab, enumerate_typed(target_ty, allowed_effects, context))
        let verified = verify_each_enriched(enriched, target_ty, cand_start)
        resume(Proposals(verified |> rank_sort(context_hole_span(context)) |> map_to_proposals, rejected))
      },
    },
  },
  verify_candidate(candidate, target_ty) => // The real proof gate (PLAN.md §1: checkpoint -> INFER -> Verify ->
  // rollback). The candidate is a real minted node in the graph; run
  // the SAME infer walk that types all source over it, read its
  // handle's binding live (NBound = the apply succeeded), AND
  // discharge the TARGET's refinement against the candidate — an
  // NBound(TInt) alone proved only well-typedness, so every integer
  // seed survived a `0 < self` target. The caller's checkpoint
  // isolates this; rollback reverts the binds.
  match candidate {
    Candidate(node, _) => // Through candidate_proven — the ONE definition, shared with the fan.
    // This arm used to carry its own copy of the conjunction, and the copy
    // had drifted: it passed the live target where the fan instantiates a
    // fresh one, so a proof here could bind the hole's own frontier. The
    // caller's checkpoint still isolates the binds.
    resume(candidate_proven(node, target_ty)),
  },
  enumerate_inhabitants(target_ty, allowed_effects, context) => {
    let enriched = enumerate_typed(target_ty, allowed_effects, context)
    // THE FAN VERIFIES FIRST (fan_verify — candidates as spawned branch
    // cursors; the arm's performs resolve OUTER, so the fan plan runs at
    // the root exactly as intended), then the per-proven resume fan
    // walks the precomputed verdicts: resume is context-bound to arms
    // (SYNTAX §resume), so the yield stays here while the proof work
    // rides the threads.
    let verdicts = fan_verify(enriched, target_ty)
    fn yield_from(i) = if i >= len(enriched) {
      resume([])
    } else {
      match list_index(enriched, i) {
        EnrichedCandidate(c) => if verdicts[i] {
          resume([c.node])
          yield_from(i + 1)
        } else {
          yield_from(i + 1)
        },
      }
    }
    yield_from(0)
  },
}

// ─── enumerate_typed — type-directed candidate enumeration ──────────
//
// The core of the enumerative proposer. Per the gradient discipline
// (PLAN §2's cached cursor): the candidate space is bounded by the
// target type + allowed-effects constraint. Each candidate is one
// way to inhabit `target_ty` under `allowed_effects`.
//
// Strategy:
//
//   1. If target is a TName (ADT), enumerate constructors.
//   2. If target is a TFun, propose lambda shapes.
//   3. If target is TInt/TFloat/String/TUnit, propose literals.
//   4. If target is TVar (unbound), propose ?? (nested hole).
//
// Each strategy yields a small set; verify_each_enriched discards
// candidates whose tentative apply doesn't unify under the
// allowed_effects row.
fn enumerate_typed(target_ty, allowed_effects, context) with EnvRead + Memory + Alloc + GraphRead + WASI + GraphWrite + LookupTy = match target_ty {
  TName(name, args) => enumerate_constructors(name, args, context),
  TFun(params, ret, row) => enumerate_lambdas(params, ret, row, context),
  TInt => enumerate_int_literals(context),
  TByte => enumerate_int_literals(context),
  TFloat => enumerate_float_literals(context),
  TUnit => one_unit_candidate(context),
  TVar(_) => one_hole_candidate(context),
  TRecord(fields) => enumerate_records(fields, context),
  TRecordOpen(fields, rest) => enumerate_records(fields, context),
  TRefined(base, _pred) => enumerate_typed(base, allowed_effects, context),
  TAlias(_, inner) => enumerate_typed(inner, allowed_effects, context),
  TReprPin(_, base) => enumerate_typed(base, allowed_effects, context),
  TCont(_, _, _, _) => one_continuation_candidate(context),
  TList(elem) => // A byte-element list IS String (the canonical alias's inner) — a
  // String-typed hole proposes string literals, never list literals.
  match seq_elem_face(elem) {
    TByte => enumerate_string_literals(context),
    _ => enumerate_list_candidates(elem, context),
  },
  TTuple(elems) => enumerate_tuple_candidates(elems, context),
}

// The Intent-Boundary pin grounding the candidate concat: the vocabulary
// list is a fold's tuple component, whose element type the graph cannot
// prove at the ++ site (the [LowExpr] precedent — the author states the
// element identity once, both operands ground).
fn concat_candidates(a: [EnrichedCandidate], b: [EnrichedCandidate]) -> [EnrichedCandidate] with Alloc + Memory =
  a ++ b

// ─── The vocabulary enumerator — the finite in-scope call space ──────
//
// Every ZERO-ARGUMENT fn in scope whose return matches the target is a
// call candidate; the ALLOWED row (the enclosing fn's authored constraint)
// gates each through the crown's own subsumption. Three outcomes per name:
//   admitted  — a call node, minted with its Why, into the proof gate;
//   rejected  — the row refused it; the (name, Reason) pair SURVIVES to
//               the projection (the rejection teaches);
//   excluded  — its free-name closure reaches the enclosing decl:
//               proposing choose() inside choose, or main() where main
//               calls choose, is nontermination by construction (silent —
//               a structural impossibility, not a teaching moment).
// Arg-bearing fns are the recursive-hole general form
// (Hβ.synth.vocabulary-arg-holes); the reach walk's O(1) form is the
// call-edge index (Hβ.synth.vocabulary-reach-index).
fn enumerate_vocabulary_calls(target_ty, allowed_row, context) with GraphRead + WASI + GraphWrite + Intern + Alloc + Memory + !Mutate = match context {
  Context(scope_bindings, _active, enclosing, hole_span) => fold(
    ([], []),
    (acc, entry) => match acc {
      (accepted, rejected) => match entry {
        (name, scheme, reason, kind) => match kind {
          FnScheme => match judgment_ty(scheme) {
            TFun(params, ret, row) => if len(params) == 0 && vocabulary_ret_matches(ret, target_ty) && !reaches_decl(name, enclosing, []) {
              if row_subsumes(row, allowed_row) {
                // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by the in-scope zero-arg vocabulary, not a loop accumulator
                (accepted ++ [vocabulary_call_candidate(name, row, allowed_row)], rejected)
              } else {
                (
                  accepted,
                  // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by the in-scope zero-arg vocabulary, not a loop accumulator
                  rejected ++ [(name, Inferred("performs {show_effrow(row)} — refused by the target row {show_effrow(allowed_row)}"))]
                )
              }
            } else {
              (accepted, rejected)
            },
            _ => (accepted, rejected),
          },
          _ => (accepted, rejected),
        },
      },
    },
    scope_bindings
  ),
}

// Return-type match for the vocabulary: refined aliases match by their
// declared identity (both anchors resolve through the SAME env alias —
// the type-level proof); unaliased grounds match structurally.
fn vocabulary_ret_matches(ret, target_ty) with Pure = match target_ty {
  TAlias(tname, _) => match ret {
    TAlias(rname, _) => rname == tname,
    _ => false,
  },
  _ => same_ground(ret, target_ty),
}

fn vocabulary_call_candidate(name, row, allowed_row) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + Intern + !Mutate + Alloc + Memory =
  EnrichedCandidate({node: synth_node(CallExpr(synth_node(VarRef(name)), [])), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("an in-scope call {name}() — row {show_effrow(row)} admitted by {show_effrow(allowed_row)}")})

// candidate_rank — the gradient reading LOCAL intent (PLAN §5's felt
// endpoint: the Reason chains carrying upstream why, proximity, the
// in-scope vocabulary). The rank is a SUM of graph reads, never a
// learned weight: the decl's nearness to the hole, plus every existing
// USE of the name weighted by its own nearness — vocabulary already
// flowing near the hole is the strongest prior; an unused far name
// sinks toward the base. The base 0.5 keeps ranked vocabulary level
// with the shape enumerators' tiers until a real signal lifts it. This
// is the field's FIRST reader — cost was a write-only literal before
// (the resume_kinds pattern: a score claiming gradient-ness that read
// nothing).
fn candidate_rank(name, decl_reason, hole_span) with GraphRead + !Mutate + Alloc + Memory = {
  let decl_near = scope_distance_decay(hole_span, caret_span_from_reason(decl_reason))
  let use_near = fold(0.0, (acc, s) => acc + scope_distance_decay(hole_span, s), refs_of_name(name))
  0.5 + decl_near + use_near
}

// rank_of — RANK IS A PROJECTION of the LIVE candidate (the resolved
// peer Hβ.synth.rank-is-a-projection-not-a-field): a named callee — a
// vocabulary or constructor call, one arm because a ctor call IS a
// CallExpr(VarRef) — ranks by the gradient's proximity read, its decl
// reason read live from the env; a nameless candidate (a lambda, a
// literal) carries the bare base with no invented differentiation (the
// stored form compared a measured proximity against enumerator
// constants, and a swapped e-class member rode a stale number).
// Computed ONCE per candidate at sort entry — the same refs walk the
// stored form paid at enrichment, moved to the read point.
fn rank_of(c, hole_span) with GraphRead + EnvRead + !Mutate + Alloc + Memory = match c {
  EnrichedCandidate(cr) => match cr.node {
    N(body, _, _) => match body {
      NExpr(CallExpr(callee, _)) => match callee {
        N(cb, _, _) => match cb {
          NExpr(VarRef(name)) => match env_lookup(name) {
            Some(entry) => match entry {
              (_, decl_reason, _) => candidate_rank(name, decl_reason, hole_span),
            },
            None => 0.5,
          },
          _ => 0.5,
        },
      },
      _ => 0.5,
    },
  },
}

fn context_hole_span(context) with Pure = match context {
  Context(_, _, _, hole_span) => hole_span,
}

// rank_sort — survivors ordered by rank, highest first: each candidate's
// rank projected once at entry, the insertion build keyed on the pair
// (the survivor set is small by construction — the in-scope zero-arg
// vocabulary plus the shape enumerators). The ORDER is the only thing
// rank decides; a constraint-tie still teaches (the render's collapse
// line is unchanged — rank is a prior, never a guess).
fn rank_sort(hole_span, cands) with GraphRead + EnvRead + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc = cands |> map((c) => (rank_of(c, hole_span), c)) |> fold([], rank_insert) |> map((rc) => match rc {
  (_, c) => c,
})

fn rank_insert(sorted, rc) with Memory + Alloc = match sorted {
  [] => [rc],
  [h, ...rest] => match rc {
    (r, _) => match h {
      (hr, _) => // drift-audit: ignore — insertion into the small survivor list, not a loop accumulator
      if r > hr { [rc] ++ sorted } else { [h] ++ rank_insert(rest, rc) },
    },
  },
}

// Does `name`'s free-name closure reach `target`? collect_free_vars
// over-approximates the call edge (a reference without a call also
// excludes — the safe direction). `seen` breaks cycles.
fn reaches_decl(name: String, target: String, seen) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc + !Mutate = if target == "" {
  false
} else if name == target {
  true
} else if contains_name(seen, name) {
  false
} else {
  match fn_body_by_name(name) {
    None => false,
    Some(body) => // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by call-graph depth (the seen set), not a loop accumulator
    any((n) => reaches_decl(n, target, [name] ++ seen), collect_free_vars(body, [])),
  }
}

fn contains_name(names, target: String) with Memory + Alloc = any((n) => n == target, names)

// A named fn's body node, read from the program weave directly — the
// same walk enclosing_fn_decl_at does, keyed by name instead of span.
// (The NModule-decls indirection read a projection the edit session's
// weave does not always carry — main() leaked past the reach exclusion
// because its body was unreachable through it; the weave is the one
// truth both walks read.)
fn fn_body_by_name(name: String) with GraphRead + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc = {
  let snapshot = graph_snapshot()
  match snapshot {
    Graph(next, _spans) => fn_body_scan(name, 0, next),
  }
}

fn fn_body_scan(name: String, i, n) with GraphRead + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc = if i >= n {
  None
} else {
  match graph_node_body(i) {
    NStmt(FnStmt(fname, _params, _ret, _effs, body)) => if fname == name { Some(body) } else { fn_body_scan(name, i + 1, n) },
    _ => fn_body_scan(name, i + 1, n),
  }
}

// ─── Per-target-shape enumerators ──────────────────────────────────
//
// The graph already proves which bindings are constructors of this
// type: each typedef's `env_extend` registered its variants as
// ConstructorScheme entries whose scheme result type is
// TName(type_name, _). Read the live env snapshot and filter — never
// a second env-access effect (the EnvLookup re-derivation is dissolved).
fn enumerate_constructors(type_name, args, context) with EnvRead + GraphRead + WASI + GraphWrite + Alloc + !Mutate + Memory = match env_snapshot() {
  Env(entries) => ctors_of_type(entries, type_name, context),
}

// Filter live env bindings for this type's constructors, minting one
// candidate per constructor. A binding is a constructor of `type_name`
// when its kind is ConstructorScheme AND its scheme's result type is
// TName(type_name, _) (nullary ctors carry TName directly; fielded ctors
// carry TFun(_, TName(type_name, _), _)) — per infer.mn:1878-1881.
// Forward walk preserves the enrichment's effect order (oldest entry
// enriched first, exactly as the rest-before-head recursion ran);
// reverse restores the newest-first result order consumers saw.
fn ctors_of_type(entries, type_name, context) with GraphRead + WASI + GraphWrite + Memory + Alloc + !Mutate = {
  let n = len(entries)
  reverse(ctors_of_type_from(entries, type_name, 0, n, make_list(n), 0))
}

fn ctors_of_type_from(entries, type_name, i, n, own buf, count) with GraphRead + WASI + GraphWrite + Memory + Alloc + !Mutate = if i >= n {
  slice(buf, 0, count)
} else {
  match list_index(entries, i) {
    (name, scheme, reason, kind) => if is_ctor_of(kind, scheme, type_name) {
      ctors_of_type_from(
        entries,
        type_name,
        i + 1,
        n,
        list_set(buf, count, ctor_to_enriched_candidate(name, scheme, reason)),
        count + 1
      )
    } else {
      ctors_of_type_from(entries, type_name, i + 1, n, buf, count)
    },
  }
}

fn ctor_to_enriched_candidate(name, scheme, reason) with GraphRead + WASI + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_ctor_call_node(name, scheme), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: VarLookup(name, reason)})

fn enumerate_lambdas(params, ret, row, context) with Alloc + Memory + GraphWrite = {
  // Single anonymous-fn candidate: (param_names) => ?? body. The
  // body becomes a nested Synth invocation; the chain re-enters
  // recursively at parse-time when the user accepts.
  let buf = make_list(1)
  let candidate =
    EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_lambda_node(params, ret), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("an anonymous fn matching the hole's shape; its body is the next hole")})
  list_set(buf, 0, candidate)
  buf
}

fn enumerate_int_literals(context) with Alloc + Memory + GraphWrite = {
  // Literal seeds: 0, 1, -1. Three candidates per the Numerical
  // Survey discipline (least-trivial inhabitants of TInt).
  let buf = make_list(3)
  list_set(buf, 0, int_literal_candidate(0))
  list_set(buf, 1, int_literal_candidate(1))
  list_set(buf, 2, int_literal_candidate(0 - 1))
  buf
}

fn int_literal_candidate(n) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_int_literal_node(n), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("the type's integer inhabitants")})

fn enumerate_float_literals(context) with Alloc + Memory + GraphWrite = {
  let buf = make_list(2)
  list_set(buf, 0, float_literal_candidate(0.0))
  list_set(buf, 1, float_literal_candidate(1.0))
  buf
}

fn float_literal_candidate(f) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_float_literal_node(f), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("the type's float inhabitants")})

fn enumerate_string_literals(context) with Alloc + Memory + GraphWrite = {
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_string_literal_node(""), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("the type's string inhabitants")})
  )
  buf
}

fn one_unit_candidate(context) with Alloc + Memory + GraphWrite = {
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_unit_literal_node(), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("the unit type's one inhabitant")})
  )
  buf
}

fn one_hole_candidate(context) with Alloc + Memory + GraphWrite = {
  // ?? at unbound positions. The recursive case: hole-fills-with-
  // hole, leaving the gradient to narrow further at the next layer.
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_hole_node(), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("a nested ?? — the shape narrows, the fill recurses")})
  )
  buf
}

fn one_continuation_candidate(context) with Alloc + Memory + GraphWrite = {
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_resume_node(), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("the continuation's own resume(...)")})
  )
  buf
}

fn enumerate_records(fields, context) with Alloc + Memory + GraphWrite = {
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_record_node(fields), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("the type's record construction")})
  )
  buf
}

fn enumerate_list_candidates(elem, context) with Alloc + Memory + GraphWrite = {
  // Two candidates: empty list, single-element with elem-hole.
  let buf = make_list(2)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_empty_list_node(), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("the type's list inhabitants — the empty list")})
  )
  list_set(
    buf,
    1,
    EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_singleton_list_node(elem), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("the type's list inhabitants — one element, itself a hole")})
  )
  buf
}

fn enumerate_tuple_candidates(elems, context) with Alloc + Memory + GraphWrite = {
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({node: synthesize_tuple_node(elems), origin: ProposerEnumerative, reason: Inferred("the type's tuple construction — each element a hole")})
  )
  buf
}

// ─── THE FAN (Arc 1) — candidates as spawned branch cursors ─────────
//
// The fused oracle's second workload: every candidate verifies as a
// REAL task over the shared image — the judgment's bracket, a candidate
// body. THE ISOLATION LAW: the sequential form was bind→rollback, net
// ZERO shared writes, so each task verifies against a FRESH
// INSTANTIATION of the target in its own planned band (the
// value-boundary law at the hole: the constraint is a published scheme;
// candidates are its callers) — race-free by construction, numbering a
// pure function of candidate order. The join replays each task's facts
// record in candidate order (branch_join whole): the fork triple never
// covered env/diag/verify state, so byte-identity with the sequential
// walk means replaying its debris exactly, proven or not.
// DONE-CRITERION (Programming by Navigation — Lubin, Ziegler & Chasins,
// PLDI 2025, defs 3.5–3.6, under the 2025-10-09 errata's covering
// reading): the fan offers ONLY candidates that lead to a valid
// completion (Strong Soundness — the proof gate) and cuts off NO
// reachable completion (Strong Completeness — a covering condition,
// never step enumeration), SCOPED to the decidable fragment
// (census-clean infer + ground-decidable Verify; V_Pending debt sits
// outside the guarantee — for undecidable validity the pair is provably
// impossible, op. cit. §8). An EMPTY fan is Fail-Fast's theorem: no
// valid completion exists from here — a teachable refusal, never a
// shrug.
let candidate_band = 2048

fn fan_verify(candidates, target_ty) = {
  let n = len(candidates)
  if n == 0 {
    []
  } else {
    let top = graph_next()
    let fan_end = top + n * (candidate_band + mint_overflow_quota)
    // Pre-open the fan's pages at the ROOT (a branch never opens a
    // band), and seal past the whole fan after the join so no later
    // root mint reuses a discarded band's stale cells (virginity).
    graph_mint_plan(fan_end)
    let base = env_base_view()
    let seed = consume_base()
    let gseed = graph_branch_seed()
    let iseed = intern_seed()
    let verdicts = fan_blocks(candidates, target_ty, base, seed, gseed, iseed, top, n, 0, [])
    graph_mint_seal(fan_end)
    verdicts
  }
}

// Blocks of judge_window, joined in candidate order — completion order
// never matters; the replay stream is byte-identical to the sequential
// walk at any block size (judge_blocks' law, second workload).
fn fan_blocks(ref candidates, target_ty, base, seed, gseed, iseed, top, n, b, own acc) = if b >= n {
  acc
} else {
  let hi = if b + judge_window < n { b + judge_window } else { n }
  let recs = fan_spawn(candidates, target_ty, base, seed, gseed, iseed, top, n, b, hi, [])
  fan_blocks(candidates, target_ty, base, seed, gseed, iseed, top, n, hi, fan_join(recs, 0, acc))
}

fn fan_spawn(ref candidates, target_ty, base, seed, gseed, iseed, top, n, j, hi, own recs) = if j >= hi {
  recs
} else {
  match candidates[j] {
    EnrichedCandidate(c) => {
      let node = c.node
      let cb = top + j * candidate_band
      let ch = top + n * candidate_band + j * mint_overflow_quota
      let range = (cb, cb + candidate_band, ch)
      // The task mirrors the root's OUTER shape (spawn_block's law): an
      // arm's performs resolve outer to its install, so renders chase
      // outside the bracket onto this empty dispatch-graph instance.
      let rec =
        spawn_task(() => candidate_judge(node, target_ty, base, seed, gseed, iseed, range) ~> graph_handler([], 0, 0, 0, [], 0, 0))
      fan_spawn(candidates, target_ty, base, seed, gseed, iseed, top, n, j + 1, hi, push(recs, rec))
    },
  }
}

fn fan_join(ref recs, j, own acc) = if j >= len(recs) {
  acc
} else {
  match join_task(recs[j]) {
    (proven, inner) => {
      branch_join(inner)
      fan_join(recs, j + 1, push(acc, proven))
    },
  }
}

// The candidate's task body under the judgment's own bracket
// (branch_bracket — one chain, two workloads). The nested trio mirrors
// the sequential verify_candidate arm exactly (its own fresh session);
// the target instantiates FRESH into this band, so the proof never
// binds the hole's live frontier.
// THE PROOF GATE, one home. §1's law is that any intelligence may propose
// and nothing executes unproven, so what "proven" MEANS has to have exactly
// one definition — and it had two. This body lived here and again in the
// sequential verify_candidate arm, whose own comment said it "mirrors" this
// one. It did not: this one instantiates the target FRESH so the proof never
// binds the hole's live frontier, and the arm passed the live target
// straight in. The dead copy was also the wrong copy, which is what a second
// home always eventually becomes.
fn candidate_proven(node, target_ty) = {
  let tcopy = instantiate(Frozen(free_in_ty(target_ty), target_ty))
  {
    infer_expr(node)
  } ~> infer_ctx ~> fresh_for_inference ~> resume_summaries_ctx
  match node {
    N(_, _, h) => verify_after_apply(h) && refinement_admits(tcopy, h),
  }
}

fn candidate_judge(node, target_ty, base, seed, gseed, iseed, range) = {
  let body = () => {
    consume_seed(seed)
    match node {
      N(_, _, _) => {
        let proven = candidate_proven(node, target_ty)
        let vdebt = verify_debt()
        let clog = consume_log()
        let pubs = env_publishes()
        let dfacts = diag_lines_taken()
        (proven, (pubs, dfacts, vdebt, clog))
      },
    }
  }
  branch_bracket(body, base, gseed, iseed, range)
}

fn keep_proven(ref cands, ref verdicts, i, own out) = if i >= len(cands) {
  out
} else {
  keep_proven(cands, verdicts, i + 1, if verdicts[i] { push(out, cands[i]) } else { out })
}

// ─── Verification — the fan gates every candidate ───────────────────
//
// The kernel's proof gate, fanned: candidates verify as spawned branch
// cursors (fan_verify above); only proven survivors reach the user.
// THE FORK/MERGE COMPOSITION, at its one seam (PLAN §5's optimality
// half + the duality): the fan has just explored MEANING-space by
// FORKING — candidates conflict, so each proved in its own rolled-back
// world, and proof is a FILTER (admit / refuse). Every survivor is then
// an equality CLASS, not one program, so the second half explores
// FORM-space by MERGING — saturation over the candidates' own range
// draws the equivalence edges, extraction picks the cost-minimal member,
// and nothing is ever undone because equals compose. The order is
// FORCED: extract-then-prove would optimize a program that may be
// inadmissible. What reaches the user is therefore proven AND
// extraction-optimal, never merely proven.
fn verify_each_enriched(candidates, target_ty, cand_start) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + LookupTy + Memory + Alloc + EnvRead + EnvWrite + Consume + Verify + Intern + WASI + WasiThreads + BranchEnv + BranchDiag + Diagnostic + !Mutate = {
  let verdicts = fan_verify(candidates, target_ty)
  let survivors = keep_proven(candidates, verdicts, 0, [])
  // The form half — only over what the fan minted (the range keeps a
  // cursor-position fan from re-saturating the whole session graph).
  saturate_range(cand_start, graph_next())
  survivors |> map(extract_optimal) |> collapse_equivalent
}

// THE FALSE TIE DISSOLVES (the duality's sharpest immediate
// consequence). §1's law is that two proven survivors cannot be picked
// between — they MEAN different things and only the author knows which,
// so the medium asks. But two survivors that extract to the SAME
// e-class do NOT mean different things: they are one meaning in two
// forms, and the tie between them is a question with no content. After
// extraction they collapse to one proposal, so the teaching tie-break
// fires only on a REAL disagreement of meaning. Keyed on the extracted
// handle — the e-class root the canon weave already carries, never a
// re-derived structural compare.
fn collapse_equivalent(cands) with Memory + Alloc = cands |> fold(
  [],
  (acc, c) => if acc |> any((k) => node_handle(candidate_node(k)) == node_handle(candidate_node(c))) {
    acc
  } else {
    acc ++ [c]
  }
)

fn candidate_node(candidate) with Pure = match candidate {
  EnrichedCandidate(c) => c.node,
}

// A survivor's node, replaced by the cost-minimal member of its own
// e-class. The extraction is a live chase of the canon weave (the same
// union-find the type layer uses); an unmoved handle keeps the node it
// already had, so a candidate with no cheaper equivalent pays nothing.
fn extract_optimal(candidate) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc = match candidate {
  EnrichedCandidate(c) => {
    let h = node_handle(c.node)
    let cheapest = egraph_extract(h)
    if cheapest == h {
      candidate
    } else {
      // The CHEAPER BODY at the CANDIDATE'S OWN SPAN: a proposal is
      // about the position the developer is filling, so taking the
      // rewritten node's span would answer a `why` with the coordinates
      // of a node they never wrote (caught in review of this fn's first
      // form). The rewrite's own justification belongs on the canon
      // EDGE — the one kernel write that carries no Reason today
      // (Hβ.egraph.canon-edge-carries-reason); until it does, the
      // candidate's seed Reason is the honest chain.
      EnrichedCandidate({node: N(graph_node_body(cheapest), node_span(c.node), cheapest), origin: c.origin, reason: c.reason})
    }
  },
}

fn node_span(node) with Pure = match node {
  N(_, span, _) => span,
}

// ─── map_to_proposals — project each survivor to its (node, reason) ──
//    pair for the Synth effect's proposal contract. The seed Reason lives
//    in the candidate record — the node's own GNode reason is only its
//    mint-span record — so dropping the pair dropped the Why from every
//    rendered survivor.
fn map_to_proposals(enriched) with Alloc + Memory = map(
  (candidate) => match candidate {
    EnrichedCandidate(c) => (c.node, c.reason),
  },
  enriched
)

// ─── Node synthesizers — per-shape AST construction ─────────────────
//
// Each helper produces a Node value the verify gate can apply to the
// graph. The actual construction reuses parser_infra's nexpr/nstmt
// constructors via the wheel's existing Node ADT (types.mn:376-379).
fn synthesize_int_literal_node(n) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(LitInt(n))

fn synthesize_float_literal_node(f) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(LitFloat(f))

fn synthesize_string_literal_node(s) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(LitString(s))

fn synthesize_unit_literal_node() with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(LitUnit)

fn synthesize_hole_node() with Memory + Alloc + GraphWrite = synth_hole_node()

fn synthesize_resume_node() with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(ResumeExpr(synth_unit_node(), []))

fn synthesize_empty_list_node() with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(MakeListExpr([]))

fn synthesize_singleton_list_node(elem) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(MakeListExpr([synth_hole_node()]))

fn synthesize_tuple_node(elems) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(MakeTupleExpr(synth_holes_for(elems)))

fn synthesize_record_node(fields) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(MakeRecordExpr(synth_record_holes_for(fields)))

fn synthesize_ctor_call_node(name, scheme) with GraphRead + WASI + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(CallExpr(synth_node(VarRef(name)), synth_holes_for_scheme(scheme)))

// params arrive as the scheme's own [TParam] — LambdaExpr's exact
// carrier; rebuilding name lists from them was the mismatch the
// converged judge caught (the param-product law reaching synth).
fn synthesize_lambda_node(params, ret) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  synth_node(LambdaExpr(params, synth_hole_node()))

// ─── Synthesis primitives — structured Node construction ───────────
//
// Every candidate node is MINTED into the one graph at a real handle
// via mint_node (parser.mn) — the same AST-in-graph fabric the parser
// births every node through. The fabricated handle-0 form was the
// load-bearing gap: a candidate with no handle cannot be inferred or
// chased, so the proof gate verified a fixed meaningless node. Minting
// edge-links the body into the graph weave so verify_candidate's
// infer_expr walk binds the candidate's own handle (Carried-Truth: the
// graph holds the node; the gate reads it live).
fn synth_node(expr_body) with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  mint_node(NExpr(expr_body), span_zero())

fn synth_unit_node() with Memory + Alloc + GraphWrite = synth_node(LitUnit)

fn synth_hole_node() with GraphRead + GraphWrite + !Mutate + Memory + Alloc =
  mint_node(NHole(0), span_zero())

fn synth_holes_for(elems) with Memory + Alloc + GraphWrite = if len(elems) == 0 {
  []
} else {
  let n = len(elems)
  let buf = make_list(n)
  synth_holes_loop(0, n, buf)
}

fn synth_holes_loop(i, n, buf) with Memory + Alloc + GraphWrite = if i >= n {
  buf
} else {
  list_set(buf, i, synth_hole_node())
  synth_holes_loop(i + 1, n, buf)
}

// A record literal's carrier is the parser's (name, value) pair list —
// bare holes lost the names (the converged judge's catch); each demanded
// field keeps its name with a hole as its value.
fn synth_record_holes_for(fields) with Memory + Alloc + GraphWrite = map(
  (f) => match f {
    (fname, _) => (fname, synth_hole_node()),
  },
  fields
)

fn synth_holes_for_scheme(scheme) with GraphRead + WASI + GraphWrite + Memory + Alloc + !Mutate = match judgment_ty(scheme) {
  TFun(params, _, _) => synth_holes_for(params),
  _ => [],
}

// ─── Composition with cursor_default + delta_default ────────────────
//
// The full Phase μ chain composes here:
//
//   cursor_session(initial_caret)
//       ~> delta_default              -- inverse-direction gradient (Hμ.gradient-delta)
//       ~> synth_default              -- proposer chain (this file)
//       ~> mentl_default              -- Teach + Why tentacles
//       ~> interrogate_default        -- automated eight-fold (Hμ.eight-interrogation-loop)
//       ~> terminal_transport         -- the human surface (Hμ.cursor.transport)
//
// The Phase μ peer handles + cursor + the kernel = the full Mentl
// active surface. ULTIMATE FORM is the medium running this chain
// continuously while the developer types — the bus-compressor closed
// at the human boundary, with proposers exploring and the gradient
// narrowing in real time. (The oracle-queue cache re-enters as graph
// content persisting with the module image — Hβ.persist.module-image-
// cache — read live by argmax, never a side-file.)
//
// ─── The closure ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Hμ.synth-proposer closes the named-but-unbodied stub at
// mentl.mn:404-438. Every `??` position in the source now invokes
// the proposer chain; every chain step gates through verify; only
// proven candidates surface to the user; the gradient narrows each
// constraint addition into a smaller candidate space until the
// surviving candidate IS the synthesized residue.
//
// Per PLAN §2's cached cursor: Mentl IS what falls out of IC plus
// Pure-over-broader-input applied to the speculative-inference loop.
// The substrate is the residue of composing what was already there.
//
// Per CLAUDE.md ⊕ + Anchor 0: wheel-canonical dream code; the seed
// transcribes after first-light-L1 + H7 MultiShot emit per Tier 3
// growth. The bus is on; the medium folds itself into its seed.
//
// Six of six Phase μ peer handles closed wheel-side. ULTIMATE FORM
// crystallizes when the developer types `??`, the chain runs, the
// argmax surfaces, the gradient narrows, the loop closes, and the
// medium teaches itself by being itself.

